WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.867 --> 00:00:02.902
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Sandra Oh.

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:03.803
-We were just
talking about Andy.

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:04.871
-We were talking about Andy.

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:05.905
When people say,
"What do you talk about

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:06.939
during the commercial breaks?"

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:08.041
We were genuinely just
sitting here

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:09.742
talking about Andy Samberg.

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:11.978
And why he's a bad guy,
about my dog.

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.747
[ Laughter ]

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:16.783
Speaking about things for a
person to be emotional about,

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.751
you have finished shooting
the final season

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:19.652
of "Killing Eve."

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:21.120
-Yes!
-Congratulations!

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.355
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:23.756
-It just started airing.

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:25.191
-Yeah!
-So, yeah.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.259
-I would imagine,
like a lot of shows

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:29.929
that had to do this during
a tricky time,

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:31.764
shooting a final season
in the middle of COVID

00:00:31.764 --> 00:00:36.102
must have tempered a little bit
how you can be celebratory.

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:38.871
-True, and also it
really, really limits

00:00:38.871 --> 00:00:39.972
how much you can travel.

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:42.608
The show is
a very international show.

00:00:42.608 --> 00:00:46.278
And it still remains that,
but this year, during COVID,

00:00:46.278 --> 00:00:48.247
you know, like many other shows,
you couldn't really travel.

00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:49.315
We couldn't really
travel that much.

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:52.184
So even though we have
exotic locations,

00:00:52.184 --> 00:00:54.454
it's exotic locations.

00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:55.988
-Like, exteriors.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:00:55.988 --> 00:00:58.357
-A lot of "exotic locations"
in the UK.

00:00:58.357 --> 00:01:00.126
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:04.063
-Did you have any sense when you
started making the show --

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:07.467
I know you'd worked on it
for awhile before it started --

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.235
that it was gonna be a hit
like this?

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.837
Because it was
a very unique show.

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:12.204
Very singular,
when it first came out.

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:13.840
-I think you can never tell.
-Yeah.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.507
-You know,
but the timing was right.

00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:16.609
The timing was right,

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:18.210
and one of the things
I'm so proud of

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.580
is the unique voice of it,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.150
and the fact that it
completely focuses on women.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.786
And complex female characters.

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:29.755
And I think our show
just hit at the right time.

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:31.791
The genre of it,
the humor of it.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.493
I feel like my and Jodie's
chemistry

00:01:34.493 --> 00:01:35.728
is really, really great.

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:39.599
So it was really emotional
and very challenging

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:42.635
to film the finale,
but it's out there now.

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:43.936
-The show is so well-written,

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:46.206
but it's hard to imagine it
with a different cast.

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:48.140
What was it like
seeing those words being read

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:51.210
by you, Jodie, Fiona, the other
people involved in the show

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.279
the first time you sat down
at a table read?

00:01:54.279 --> 00:01:55.882
-You must know it, right?

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:58.117
It's like, when you do
a table read, right,

00:01:58.117 --> 00:01:59.986
which you've done
a bajillion times,

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:01.620
it's the crackle.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.589
-It's that crackle,
and you can feel.

00:02:03.589 --> 00:02:06.559
It's a mysterious thing,
but it happens a lot in theater,

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.628
when everyone in the room
can feel like,

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.463
"Oh, it's happening.
It's happening."

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:12.098
And not only that,
the next steps,

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:13.132
you're gonna have
with great actors.

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:14.667
You're gonna put that on set,
on film.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.903
I mean, for me, I remember,
like,

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:20.473
particularly Fiona Shaw,
who plays Carolyn,

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:24.309
I completely, utterly fangirled
out when I first met her.

00:02:24.309 --> 00:02:25.578
I don't know if you know,

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:28.614
but she is a remarkable
theater actress.

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:29.782
She's a very, very well-known,

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.517
very well-regarded
theater actress.

00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:35.154
And I saw her in "Medea,"
on Broadway in 2003.

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:37.256
Right? I don't know
if you know the play...

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:38.725
-That's the real deal.
-Right? The real deal.

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:42.261
So when I met her, I did
the very, very last moment.

00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:43.462
I'm like, come on.

00:02:43.462 --> 00:02:45.497
It's like, "Hi, Fiona.

00:02:45.497 --> 00:02:46.532
I saw you in 'Medea.'

00:02:46.532 --> 00:02:47.633
This was your last moment."

00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:48.701
And acted it out for her.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.936
-People love that.
-Right? [ Laughs ]

00:02:50.936 --> 00:02:54.240
But we're friends now.
-Okay, good. It worked out.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.774
I have one more thing
to ask you about.

00:02:55.774 --> 00:02:57.443
So you have a new film
coming out,

00:02:57.443 --> 00:03:00.113
which seems like, very different
than Disney Pixar.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:01.981
-Yes, also a mother-daughter
relationship.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:04.183 line:0%
-This is "Umma."
-Yes.

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:06.619 line:0%
-This looks terrifying.
-[ Laughing ]

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:08.554 line:0%
It is,
it's a psychological horror

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:11.257 line:0%
that is also based on
a complex relationship

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:12.959 line:0%
between a mother and a daughter.

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:15.828
-It's a rich vein,
those relationships.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.296
-It is.

00:03:17.296 --> 00:03:20.099
I do really like playing it,
you know, again.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:21.901
It's two young, female,

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.036
actually Asian American
filmmakers,

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:26.806
who are like,
exploring stories about mother.

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:28.808
You know,
it's the primary storytelling.

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:30.076
-That's great.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:32.011
Hey, it is always just
such a delight to see you.

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.612
Thank you so much for being here
in person.

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:34.714
-Thank you.
-You guys.

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:36.082
[ Cheers and applause ]
That's Sandra Oh.

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:38.418
"Turning Red" is streaming this
Friday on Disney Plus.

